    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d94">

                <pb facs="0093" n="85" sameAs="frus:frus1941v07/pg85"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">865.85/258a: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Acting Secretary of
                            State to the Minister in Costa Rica</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Hornibrook</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1941-03-30T13:00:00-05:00">March 30, 1941—1 p.m.</date></dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>45. Department’s 43, March 28, 2 p.m. and your 51, March 29, 10 a.m.<anchor n="7"
                        xml:id="d94-f1"/> Please at once seek an interview with the President and
                    give him the information contained in the following circular telegram which is
                    being sent to all our chiefs of mission in the other American Republics:</p>
                <p>[Here follows text of circular telegram of March 30, printed in <ref
                        target="frus:frus1941v06/pg_185">volume VI, page 185</ref>.]</p>
                <p>In view of these circumstances, the suggestion contained in the final sentence of
                    the Department’s No. 43 of March 28, 2 p.m., is no longer pertinent. The
                    Department will be interested in learning of <pb facs="0094" n="86"
                        xml:id="pg86"/> the decision of the Costa Rican Government regarding the two
                    vessels at Puntarenas. The situation which has developed in the United States
                    illustrates the desirability of maximum precautionary measures.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Welles</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
